From: win4wine
Subject: [PATCH] win4wine: the XP shell's session compositor

Purpose: labwc as the window manager of win4wine's Wayland session: the
XP shell (Wine on Xwayland) at the outputs' own resolution, with
per-output and fractional scales, and Linux applications (Wayland and X11)
with genuine XP frames, as bridge-wm does on X11.

Xwayland at the outputs' resolution (xwayland-scale.[ch]):
- Xwayland gets an xdg-output manager of its own: each output at its size
  in pixels, side by side ("X space"), the primary output first so that
  Win32's 0,0 is its top-left.
- X windows are placed in layout coordinates by the scale of the output
  they are on and shown 1/scale (wlroots patch 0001), one part per output
  for a window across outputs of different scales. Configure requests,
  size hints, the work area (_NET_WORKAREA, _GTK_WORKAREAS_D0) and the
  pointer outside a pressed X window are converted between the two.
- Xwayland gets no relative pointer: it sends buttons from that device,
  whose position does not follow absolute motion (tablets, VMs).

The bridge (win4wine.[ch], enabled by WIN4WINE_IPC): bridge-wm's IPC with
proxy.exe (windows.tsv, geometry.tsv, frames.tsv, *.cmd, switch.tsv,
shellkeys.tsv, icons); XP frames below their windows, moved, resized,
maximized and minimized with them; Wine's identity (_WIN4WINE_CLASS,
_WIN4WINE_HWND) on an X connection of its own; XP's desktop at the bottom
and the taskbar, the Start menu and topmost windows above; X's stacking
following the scene; Alt+Tab with XP's switch window, the Windows key and
Ctrl+Esc under Linux windows; the taskbar's edge of the work area; Wine
choosing the window to activate when one of its windows goes, and
mapped windows activated as Wine marks them (_NET_WM_USER_TIME); the active
Linux window's frame shown active through _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW only; Wine's
unmanaged windows (the ReactOS Start menu, tooltips) taking no keyboard
when they map, and X's focus given back to the Wine window that has the
keyboard when it falls to no window.

As bridge-wm does, also for Windows 7's shell:
- X windows show only their bounding shape (X's SHAPE extension, read on
  the bridge's X connection; wlroots patch 0003): Wine's window regions,
  such as the rounded corners of XP's and Windows 7's frames and the
  unused part of Windows 7's Start menu, were black.
- A Linux window without a position of its own takes Windows' next
  default-window slot (wm.py's cascade); one maximized before its frame's
  borders were known is fitted again when they are.
- The work area follows the taskbar's size and place (Windows 7's small
  icons); Wine's own windows are left out of labwc's rearranging, since
  Wine places them and fits its maximized windows itself. The edges the
  taskbar sets come from its X geometry, exactly (through layout
  coordinates they were one pixel into the taskbar on a fractional scale).
- On a fractional scale an X window is drawn at its exact pixel position
  on the output (wlroots patch 0003), not one pixel off.
- Each Linux window's application (its app_id, or WM_CLASS's class) goes
  to classes.tsv, as bridge-wm writes it: the proxy groups the windows of
  an application under one taskbar button of Windows 7's and gives them
  that application's jump list title and pinning (each window had a
  button of its own).
- Frames with something to draw come no more often than the output
  refreshes. A display that ends page flips at once (a virtual machine's
  VGA) drew a frame for each change, as often as the CPU allowed (77 a
  second while a window was dragged on the test VM's 75 Hz output).
- An X window is shown once its client drew (its surface's next commit
  after the one that mapped it, or after 100 ms), not with the blank black
  buffer Xwayland commits at map; the scene's hidden surface still gets
  frame events, so that Xwayland goes on committing. Windows 7's folder
  windows and Start menu showed one or two black frames as they opened.
